nodecode-glasses is a rewrite of 100yenadmin's 100yenadmin/glasses-platform:

  https://github.com/100yenadmin/glasses-platform
  commit dbc797b6cc4063261c5be298288e599322913b98
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Electric Sheep (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the whole channel, in Common Lisp against the Nodecode cell
contract. A device still authenticates with its own token, one per wearer;
a query still becomes one turn in that wearer's own session; answers still
come back as short lens lines, split at a length and stripped of markdown;
a visual question still takes a fresh photo first, with the original's
keyword and deictic rules kept word for word; the camera is still a tool the
model may call, and a capture request still waits for the device to answer;
frames said while a device was away are still held in a queue with a
ceiling, as the original's in-memory outbox was; and the routes, the
reminder push and the session reset of the original's protocol are kept as
verbs a client can call.

Left out: the WebSocket transport. The original served ws:// on a port of
its own, with two protocol versions, streamed deltas, status frames and a
per-turn reply state machine inside the Hermes gateway. A cell does not own
a port beside the gateway, so the rewrite is HTTPS-on-the-gateway-port with
a poll: /hello, /say, /poll and /photo answer the same facts as frames, and
streaming was dropped in favour of the finished answer, because a lens shows
one line at a time anyway. A companion app written against the original's
WebSocket frames needs a small adapter, not a rewrite. Also left out:
Hermes' own cron wrapper unwrapping and its approval-prompt fan-out, which
are the harness's business, not a cell's.
